          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The formal notion of
            %em curvature
            is a formalization and generalization of the intuitive notion of the (“
            %a{:href => "#Extrinsic"}> extrinsic
            ”) curvature of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            embedded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            This extrinsic curvature of a surface is called
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Gaussian curvature
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Gaussian+curvature"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            that it is embedded in: the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            on the surface and the surface’s curvature is encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of the surface.
          %p
            This notion of curvature of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            in turn generalizes straightforwardly to a notion of curvature of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            and thus gives the name to the general concept.
          %p
            For instance in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order formulation of gravity
            the curvature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is literally the curvature of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            on spacetime in this sense. But also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
            is a connection on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            and its curvature encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+field"} Yang-Mills field
          %p
            Even more generally, the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            and of a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#OfInfLieAlgebroidValuedForms"} curvature of ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
          %p
            In
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} Eilenberg-Steenrod
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            describing
            %em abelian
            such higher connections these curvatures appear in the form of generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+characteristic+form"} curvature characteristic form
          %h2#Extrinsic Extrinsic curvature of embedded manifolds
          %p
            Curvature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa(\gamma)
            of a smooth curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            at a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            of a smooth curve is the (signed) inverse radius of the (oriented) circle having tangency of order 1 with the curve at the point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frenet-Serret+formulas"} Frenet-Serret formulas
            express the derivative of Frenet moving frame with respect to the parameter of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturally+parametrized+curve"} naturally parametrized curve
            in
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Gaussian curvature
          %p
            %strong Sectional curvature
            of a higher dimensional smooth surface at its point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in an Euclidean space along a tangent 2-dimensional plane is the Gaussian curvature of the curve which is the intersection of the surface with the plane. As a plane is determined by two vectors, the sectional curvature is determined by a surface and a pair of vectors, and all possible sectional curvatures can be read form that 2-form; therefore we talk about the operator of the curvature.
          %p
            Curvature can be described also intrinsically, without recourse to the ambient space and its metric. Therefore it makes sense in Riemannian geometry based on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            tensor just on a manifold. In 1917, Herman Weyl postulated a more fundamental quantity than a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            on a fibre bundle, giving hence rise to a modern, generalized idea of the curvature. While Riemannian metric gives rise to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of the Riemannian manifold, not every connection on a vector or principal bundles is induced by metrics. In that sense the connection is a more basic notion in geometry.
          %h2#curvature_of_a_connection Curvature of a connection
          %p
            The
            %em curvature
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            measures how the connection is
            %em locally
            non-trivial.
          %p
            In as far as the notion of connection on a bundle is generalized by the notion of a cocycle in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
          %p
            In as far as cocycles in differential cohomology represent
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of these gauge fields.
          %p
            After the conception of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %em curvature
            was firmly established in its generalization from this special case to the case of connections on all kinds of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"} higher bundles
          %h3#OfLieAlgebraValuedForm Curvature of a Lie-algebra valued form
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A connection on a trivial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is just a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} 1-form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      A \in \Omega^1(X)
                      \\,.
              %p
                The curvature in this case is the 2-form
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo =
                        %mi d
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = d A
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                on a trivial
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
                is a
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+of+Lie-algebra+valued+forms"} groupoid of Lie-algebra valued forms
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      A \in \Omega^1(X) \otimes \mathfrak{g}
                      \\,.
              %p Its curvature is the Lie-algebra valued 2-form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi d
                      %mi A
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      F_A = \mathbf{d} A + [A \wedge A]
                      \\,,
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
                is the Lie bracket in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %li
              %p
                According to the discussion at
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
                is given by a collection of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#OfInfLieAlgebroidValuedForms"} curvature of ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
          %h3#curvature_characteristic_forms Curvature characteristic forms
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+characteristic+form"} curvature characteristic form
          %h3#geometric_interpretation_of_curvature_2forms Geometric interpretation of curvature 2-forms
          %p
            For the geometric interpretation of the curvature 2-form of a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\leftarrow
                  W(\mathfrak{g})
                  \:
                  A
          %p
            it is useful to recall that both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+complex"} deRham complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet(X)
            as well as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            are naturally interpreted as function algebras on
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+complex"} deRham complex
            may be thought of as the algebra of functions on the infinitesimal path ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Π
                      %mi inf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^{inf}(X)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            paths
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \to y
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  x &amp;\to&amp; y_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; \searrow &amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  y_2 &amp;\to&amp; z
                  }
          %p
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            On the other hand,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(\mathfrak{g})
            is the algebra of functions on the infinitesimal version
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G_{(1)}
            of what is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi λ
                        %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi a
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {*} \stackrel{e + \lambda^a t_a}{\to} {*}
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            the neutral element of the group (the identity), for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_a
            an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            as before and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi λ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda^a
            some coefficient.
          %p A 2-morphism is an infinitesimal surface bounded by such infinitesimal 1-morphisms such that going either way around the surface
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi e
                              %mo +
                              %msubsup
                                %mi λ
                                %mn 1
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi λ
                                %mn 3
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi λ
                                %mn 2
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msubsup
                                %mi λ
                                %mn 4
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  {*} &amp;\stackrel{e + \lambda^a_1 t_a}{\to}&amp; {*}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\rlap{\lambda_3^a t_a}}
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp; \downarrow^{\rlap{\lambda_2^a t_a}}
                  \\\
                  {*} &amp;\stackrel{\lambda_4^a t_a}{\to}&amp; {*}
                  }
          %p produces the same result when then morphisms are composed using the product in the Lie group: the top right way around the square here yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 1
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 1
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 1
                    %mi a
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (e + \lambda_1^a t_a) )(e + \lambda_2^b t_b) )
                  \=
                  e + \lambda_1^a t_a + \lambda_2^b t_b
                  +
                  \\lambda_1^a \lambda_2^b t_a t_b
          %p and the other way round yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 3
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 4
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 3
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 4
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 3
                    %mi a
                  %msubsup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 4
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (e + \lambda_3^a t_a) )(e + \lambda_4^b t_b) )
                  \=
                  e + \lambda_3^a t_a + \lambda_4^b t_b
                  +
                  \\lambda_3^a \lambda_4^b t_a t_b
                  \\,.
          %p
            A morphism of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X) \leftarrow CE(\mathfrak{g})
                  \:
                  A
          %p is now evidently equivalenty a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi inf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A : \Pi^{inf}(X) \to \mathbf{B}G_{(1)}
          %p
            that sends infinitesimal paths in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to infinitesimal group elements of the form
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi λ
                      %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e + \lambda^a t_a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A : (x \to y) \;\;\mapsto\;\;
                  ({*} \stackrel{e + A^a(x,y) t_a}{\to} {*})
                  \\,.
          %p If we denote by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v = y - x
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector"} tangent vector
            that connects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimally
            close points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(x,y) = A_x(v)
            as a function of the first point and the vector pointing away from it, then this reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo +
                      %msubsup
                        %mi A
                        %mi x
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi a
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A : (x \to y) \;\;\mapsto\;\;
                  ({*} \stackrel{e + A^a_x(v) t_a}{\to} {*})
                  \\,.
          %p
            We can now look at what this assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of infinitesimal group elements to infinitesimal paths does to a little square in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as above, with sides spanned by
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi v
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi v
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi y
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi e
                              %mo +
                              %msub
                                %mi A
                                %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi v
                                %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi A
                                %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi v
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi A
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi y
                                    %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi v
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi A
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi y
                                    %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi v
                                %mn 1
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mi a
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mi a
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A
                  \\;\;
                  \:
                  \\;\;
                  \\array{
                  x &amp;\stackrel{v_1}{\to}&amp; y_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{v_2}
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  y_2 &amp;\to &amp; z
                  }
                  \\;\;\mapsto\;\;
                  \\array{
                  {*} &amp;\stackrel{e + A_x(v_1) t_a}{\to}&amp; {*}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\rlap{A_x(v_2)^a t_a}}
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;
                  \\downarrow^{\rlap{A_{y_1}(v_2)^a t_a}}
                  \\\
                  {*} &amp;\stackrel{A_{y_2}(v_1)^a t_a}{\to}&amp; {*}
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            For the result on the right to qualify as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} 2-morphism
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G_{(1)}
            we need that
          %p going around the top right edges, which yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  e + A_x^a(v_1) t_a + A^b_{y_1}(v_2) t_b
                  +
                  A_x^a(v_1) A^b_{y_1}(v_2) t_a t_b
          %p is the same as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  e + A_x^a(v_2) t_a + A^b_{y_2}(v_1) t_b
                  +
                  A_{x}^a(v_2) A^b_{y_2}(v_1) t_a t_b
                  \\,.
          %p
            To express what this means as a condition at the point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A_{y_1} = A_x + \partial_{v_1} A_x
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A_{y_2} = A_x + \partial_{v_2} A_x
                  \\,.
          %p Then some terms cancel and the above condition becomes, to second order
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial_{v_1} A^a_x(v_2) t_a
                  +
                  A_x^a(v_1) A^b_{x}(v_2) t_a t_b
                  \=
                  \\partial_{v_2} A^a_x(v_1) t_a
                  +
                  A_x^a(v_2) A^b_{x}(v_1) t_a t_b
                  \\,.
          %p In other words, the expression
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_A(v_1,v_2)
                  \:=
                  \\partial_{v_1} A^a_x(v_2) t_a
                  \-
                  \\partial_{v_2} A^a_x(v_1) t_a
                  +
                  \\frac{1}{2} A_x^a(v_1) A^b_{x}(v_2) [t_a, t_b]
          %p has to vanish. This is the curvature form that we already found above by more algebraic means.
          %p
            If this does not vanish, then we don’t really have a morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Π
                      %mi inf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : \Pi^{inf}(X) \to \mathbf{B}G_{(1)}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^a
            to assigns data to little edges,
            %em and
            that uses the 2-forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_A^a
            to assign data to little surfaces. That morphism then will respect a condition as above, but now on little cubes. That condition is the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d F_A + [A \wedge F_A] = 0
          %p on the curvature 2-form.
          %h2#OfInfLieAlgebroidValuedForms
            Curvature of
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The notion of curvature of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-algebra+valued+1-form"} Lie-algebra valued 1-form
            discussed
            %a{:href => "#OfLieAlgebraValuedForm"} above
            generalizes to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid+valued+differential+forms"} ∞-Lie algebroid valued differential forms
          %h3#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            be an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra"} ∞-Lie algebra
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X) \leftarrow
                  W(\mathfrak{g})
                  \: A
          %p
            of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet(X)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(\mathfrak{g})
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
          %p
            There is a canonical inclusion of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W(\mathfrak{g}) \leftarrow \mathfrak{g}^*[1] : F_{(-)}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %strong
              curvature of the
              = succeed "-Lie" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi A
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X) \stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}
                  W(\mathfrak{g})
                  \\stackrel{F_{(-)}}{\leftarrow}
                  \\mathfrak{g}^*[1]
                  \:
                  F_A
                  \\,.
          %p
            This consists, in general, of a tower of components: write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_n
            for the degree
            = succeed "-part" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi A
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mi k
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X) \stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}
                  W(\mathfrak{g})
                  \\stackrel{F_{(-)}}{\leftarrow}
                  \\mathfrak{g}_{k}^*[1]
                  \:
                  (F_A)_{k+1}
                  \\,.
          %p So
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (F_A)_n
                  \\in
                  \\Omega^n(X, \mathfrak{g}_{n-1})
          %p
            is a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_{n-1}
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            One may speak of the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong 2-form curvature
            = succeed "," do
              %strong 3-form curvature
            %strong 4-form curvature
            and so on.
          %p
            If instead of just an
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            we take more generally an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 1-form curvature component
          %p
            %strong Remark
            In some places in the literature, the lower curvature form components have been called
            %em fake curvature
            (
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0106083"}> BreenMessing
            ).
          %h3#properties Properties
          %h4#obstruction_to_flatness Obstruction to flatness
          %p
            Precisely if the curvatures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_A
            vanish does the morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : W(\mathfrak{g}) \to \Omega^\bullet(X)
            factor through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi CE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(\mathfrak{g}) \to CE(\mathfrak{g})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi CE
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∃
                                  %msub
                                    %mi A
                                    %mi flat
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mo •
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mi W
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (F_A = 0)
                  \\;\;\Leftrightarrow
                  \\;\;
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; CE(\mathfrak{g})
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\exists A_{flat}}}\swarrow &amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X) &amp;\stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}&amp; W(\mathfrak{g})
                  }
                  \\right)
          %p
            in which case we call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %strong flat
          %h4#bianchi_identity Bianchi identity
          %p
            By the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{dR} F_A + A(d_{W(\mathfrak{g})}(-)) = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
          %h3#curvature_characteristic_forms_2 Curvature characteristic forms
          %p
            The algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi inv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} inv(\mathfrak{g})
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi inv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W(\mathfrak{g}) \leftarrow inv(\mathfrak{g})
                  \\,.
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi inv
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle - \rangle \in inv(\mathfrak{g})
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi A
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                  %mi inv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(X)
                  \\stackrel{A}{\leftarrow}
                  W(\mathfrak{g})
                  \\stackrel{}{\leftarrow}
                  inv(\mathfrak{g})
                  \\stackrel{\langle - \rangle}{\leftarrow}
                  CE(b^{n-1} \mathbb{R})
                  \:
                  \\langle F_A \rangle
          %p
            is the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+characteristic+form"} curvature characteristic form
